{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-351199",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Our task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{6x-11}{(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-4}+\\frac{B}{(x-4)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-4)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-11 & =A(x-4)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=4$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(4)-11 & =A(4-4)+B \\\\ 13 & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ and using our value for $B$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(0)-11 & =A(0-4)+13 \\\\ -11 & =-4A+13 \\\\ -4A & =-24 \\\\ A & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{6x-11}{(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{6}{x-4}+\\frac{13}{(x-4)^{2}}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
